Description: Let a modifier tapped on its own send a key combination.
 Mutter already treats a lone tap of two modifiers specially: the overlay key opens the overview and the
 locate-pointer key shows the pointer. This adds a third, configured with tap-key and tap-key-sends in
 org.gnome.mutter. When the tap-key modifier is pressed and released on its own, the accelerator in
 tap-key-sends is sent in its place. Pressed together with other keys it stays an ordinary modifier.
 .
 The tap detection is the one the other two keys use, including the reset on pointer buttons and the check
 for windows inhibiting shortcuts. The combination is sent through a virtual keyboard, so it passes through
 keybinding processing like a key pressed by hand and reaches keybindings and global shortcuts.
 .
 The test in src/tests/keybindings.c holds the tap key together with another key, which must send nothing,
 and then taps it on its own, which must trigger a keybinding bound to the combination it sends.
